# Supporting Data releases under cdsi-reference are checksummed byte-for-byte
# in their manifest.yaml (see supporting_data.py) - git's default line-ending
# normalization (core.autocrlf) would silently rewrite the XML/XSD text files
# on checkout, breaking those checksums on any machine with a different
# autocrlf setting than the one that originally staged them. Treat the whole
# preserved source tree as binary so it round-trips byte-for-byte everywhere.
cdsi-reference/supporting-data/versions/**/source/** binary

# FITS fixture JSON files are checksummed byte-for-byte in reference-set.json
# (see ReferenceSetVerifier.computeFixtureSet, kept in lockstep with
# cdsi-reference's reference_sets.py) so a FITS run can trust it hasn't
# silently drifted. Same autocrlf hazard as above - a fixture committed with
# LF checks out as CRLF wherever core.autocrlf=true, which changes its
# sha256 without changing its content and breaks that checksum. Treat these
# as binary so they round-trip byte-for-byte on every machine.
cdsi-fits-tests/src/test/resources/fits/** binary
